problemStatement:
Given the HTML `<p id="greeting" class="message">Hello, Progly!</p>`, you have the following three CSS rules in a stylesheet (in this order):

```css
p {
  color: green;
}
.message {
  color: blue;
}
#greeting {
  color: red;
}
```

**Questions (answer in a comment in your CSS):**
1. What colour will the text be and why?
2. If you add `p { color: orange !important; }` at the very end, what colour now?

Now, write a fourth rule that changes the text colour to purple **without using `!important`** and without modifying the existing three rules. Where would you place it to ensure it wins? Write the CSS rule and explain in a comment.

Finally, write a CSS snippet for a `<style>` block that demonstrates inheritance: set `body` to have `color: darkgray;` and `font-family: Arial;`. Then override the colour for a specific paragraph with class `special` to be black. Show the HTML and CSS together.

solutionCode:
/* 1. The text will be red. #greeting (ID) has highest specificity (0,1,0) vs .message (0,0,1) vs p (0,0,0).
2. With !important on p { color: orange !important; } at the end, it becomes orange because !important overrides normal rules regardless of specificity. */

/* To make it purple without !important, use a more specific selector, e.g., #greeting.message { color: purple; }.
   Place it after the existing rules to take advantage of equal specificity and source order? Actually this has higher specificity (0,1,1) so it wins regardless of order. */

#greeting.message {
  color: purple;
}

/* ---- Inheritance example ---- */
/* HTML:
<body>
  <p>Normal paragraph.</p>
  <p class="special">Special paragraph.</p>
</body>
*/
body {
  color: darkgray;
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}
.special {
  color: black;
}
/* The first paragraph inherits darkgray. The .special paragraph overrides to black. */
